      "text": "Let's construct the statement: \\[\\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2\\]Let \\[\\sqrt[3]{x} = y\\]Then in this case, we get \\[y + \\sqrt[3]{20-x} = 2\\]This means that \\[\\sqrt[3]{20-x} = \\sqrt{2} - y\\]Using the fact that $y^3 = x$ and $(20 - x)^3 = (20 - y^3)^3$ we can get: \\[20 - y^3 - (20-3y^3-3y\\sqrt{20-2y^3}-y^3\\sqrt{20-2y^3}) = 243-726\\cdot2-y^3-3y\\sqrt{20-2y^3}-y^3\\sqrt{20-2y^3}\\]Simplifying and solving for y we get: \\[20-4y^3+4\\sqrt{2}y=243-240y-2y^3\\]\n\nSolving this, we are told that the values of $y$ are $y=\\sqrt2-\\frac12$ and $y=\\sqrt2+\\frac12$, which are the two real roots of the expression. Then $x=(\\sqrt2-\\frac12)^3,\\ (\\sqrt2+\\frac12)^3$. Obviously, we are looking for the smallest root. Since $\\sqrt2\\approx 1.4$ we have $2\\sqrt2\\approx 2.8$, so we need to calculate: \\[2.8^3-3\\cdot2.8^2+4\\cdot2.8^3-4\\cdot2.8=4\\cdot2.8^2\\]\n\nThen $x=\\sqrt[3]{2^9}(2^2-2\\sqrt2+\\frac{1}{8})=\\sqrt[3]1(319/8)=319/8=(29)^3/(2)=-29+\\sqrt{841}\\] Since $p=-29$ and $q=841$, we therefore have $p+q=810$. The answer is: 810",
